  "question": {
    "text": "For different values of $\\alpha$, the locus of the point of intersection of the two straight lines $\\sqrt{3} x-y-4 \\sqrt{3} \\alpha=0$ and $\\sqrt{3} \\alpha x+\\alpha y-4 \\sqrt{3}=0$ is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "a hyperbola with eccentricity 2",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "an ellipse with eccentricity $\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "a hyperbola with eccentricity $\\sqrt{\\frac{19}{16}}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "an ellipse with eccentricity $\\frac{3}{4}$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Hints : $\\sqrt{3} x-y=4 \\sqrt{3} \\alpha$\n(1) $\\sqrt{3} x+y=\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{\\alpha}$\n(1) $x(2) \\Rightarrow 3 x^2-y^2=48 \\Rightarrow \\frac{x^2}{16}-\\frac{y^2}{48}=1$\n$$\n\\mathrm{e}=\\sqrt{\\frac{48+16}{16}}=2\n$$"
  }
